{
  "frozen_time": "2026-09-21 11:05:00 PDT",
  "user_scenario": "Harold Deakins spent thirty-one years under the bonnets of Marwick's buses and has never once enjoyed a telephone call to an insurance company. He is standing just inside the pharmacy on Ivyhurst Road at eleven in the morning, coat still on, because the girl behind the counter has told him she cannot hand over his new tablets and that he had better ring the plan himself. He is not angry with her. He is, faintly, angry with the telephone.\n\nWhat he wants is simple and he says it in the first breath: the chemist is holding a prescription for him, he would like whatever is stopping it dealt with, and then he would like to pay for it and go home. He is quite clear that he wants to settle it today and at this counter rather than come back, and he is equally clear that he is not leaving until somebody tells him a number. He says he will put it on his own card himself when the time comes; he does not want anybody putting it through on his behalf without him seeing the figure first.\n\nAsked who he is, he gives his name as Harold Deakins and his date of birth as 1948-11-02. If the number on the Medicare card is wanted he reads it out as 2KW9RT3HQ48, slowly, because he has to hold it at arm's length. His telephone number is 3127760453 and his address is 62 Ivyhurst Road, Marwick, if either is asked for. He is content for the agent to look at anything on his file and says as much without being asked twice. Nobody acts for him and there is nobody else with him.\n\nHe does not know what the tablets cost. If he is asked he says the pharmacist mentioned a figure once and he did not take it in, and that the agent had better read it off whatever they can see rather than go by him. He cannot say what he has spent on his prescriptions this year either; he pays what he is asked and keeps none of the slips. If he is asked about the card the plan sends him for the chemist and the groceries, he says he has it in his wallet and uses it for the odd bottle of paracetamol, and he has no idea what is left on it.\n\nOnce he is told what he has to hand over he does not argue about the amount, though he may mutter that it is a good deal more than the last lot. When the agent passes the payment over for him to make, he makes it, and if he is asked to confirm it afterwards he reads back what comes up on the receipt. If a figure is quoted to him and then nothing happens, he waits a beat and asks whether he can pay it now or whether he is meant to do something else.\n\nWhen he hears that the fill has gone through and the pharmacist can hand the bag over, he says that is all he wanted, thanks the agent rather more warmly than he began, and puts the telephone away.",
